WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 295: Henry I: The White Ship Disaster, the Loss of the Heir, and the Lion of Justice

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:20.000
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the Scholar King, whose administrative brilliance established English royal justice, but whose dynastic dreams were swallowed by the freezing waters of the English Channel.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of King Henry I.

5
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:37.600
[George] Born in Yorkshire in late 1068, Henry was the fourth and youngest son of William the Conqueror and Queen Matilda, and the only son born on English soil after the conquest.

6
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:47.360
[George] Endowed with a scholarly mind and educated in Latin, law and liberal arts in Royal Abbeys, he was nicknamed Beauclerc, meaning fine scholar.

7
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.120
[George] When the conqueror died in 1087, Robert received Normandy and Rufus took England.

8
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.520
[George] Henry received 5,000 silver pounds and not an inch of land.

9
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:05.120
[George] For thirteen years, Henry navigated the violent feuds between his older brothers with ice-cold patience and calculated cunning.

10
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:14.000
[George] When Rufus was struck down by Tyle's arrow in the New Forest in August 1100, 31-year-old Henry acted with ruthless decisiveness.

11
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:29.760
[George] Galloping to Winchester, he drew his sword on Royal Treasurer William of Bretuwil, seized the royal gold, rode to Westminster, and was crowned King Henry I on August 5, barely 72 hours after Rufus' blood had soaked the bracken.

12
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:46.000
[George] Henry's childhood as the landless younger prince in the shadow of his older brothers forced him to develop a cold, calculating intellect and a patient mastery of human psychology that proved far deadlier than the impetuous battlefield bravado of Robert Curthos or William Rufus.

13
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:59.520
[George] Educated in the Royal Abbey of Abingdon, Henry mastered reading and writing in Latin, studied the complexities of canon and civil law, and learned how to audit financial accounts, earning the admiring sobriquet Beauclerc.

14
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:08.200
[George] His swift, ruthless seizure of the treasury at Winchester on August 2, 1100, drew praise from administrative chroniclers.

15
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:16.120
[George] Henry knew that in medieval statecraft, possession of the royal crown and silver coin was nine-tenths of legitimate sovereign power.

16
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.160
[George] Henry's accession was a brazen usurpation.

17
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:25.480
[George] His eldest brother Robert Curthos was returning from the Crusades with a superior legal claim to the throne.

18
00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:34.600
[George] To win over the English Church and the suspicious baronage, Henry issued his landmark coronation charter, known to history as the Charter of Liberties.

19
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.400
[George] In this historic document, Henry explicitly renounced Rufus's abuses.

20
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.600
[George] He promised not to plunder vacant church lands.

21
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:54.280
[George] Regulated feudal inheritance taxes, and pledged to restore the ancient laws of Edward the Confessor, providing the direct ideological framework that inspired Magna Carta a century later.

22
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.400
[George] Henry then performed a political masterstroke.

23
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:07.720
[George] In November 1100, he married Princess Matilda of Scotland, daughter of King Malcolm III and Saint Margaret, a direct descendant of King Alfred the Great.

24
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:15.400
[George] By uniting the Norman conquerors with the ancient Saxon royal bloodline, Henry won the fervent devotion of the English people.

25
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:24.120
[George] Henry's Charter of Liberties, issued at his coronation on August 5, 1100, was a revolutionary constitutional milestone.

26
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:34.200
[George] By voluntarily binding the royal prerogative under the rule of written law, Henry established the profound legal principle that the King of England was not above the law.

27
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] His marriage to Edith of Scotland, who was renamed Queen Matilda upon her coronation, was an emotional and political masterstroke that healed the deep ethnic trauma of 1066.

28
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:57.840
[George] As the daughter of Saint Margaret, Queen Matilda carried the sacred blood of Alfred the Great, allowing their children to unite the ancient Saxon royal house of Surdic with the Marshal Norman bloodline of the conqueror.

29
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:08.240
[George] When Robert Curthos landed an invasion force at Portsmouth in 1101, Henry outmaneuvered his brother with money and diplomacy, buying him off with an annual pension.

30
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:15.440
[George] But Duke Robert's chaotic, incompetent rule in Normandy allowed banditry and civil war to spread across the Channel.

31
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.600
[George] In 1106, Henry took the offensive, sailing to Normandy with a seasoned English army.

32
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:32.000
[George] On September 28, 1106, forty years to the exact day since the conqueror landed at Pevensey, Henry crushed Robert's army at the Battle of Tinchabrae.

33
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.080
[George] Capturing his older brother on the field, Henry reunited England and Normandy under a single crown.

34
00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:52.240
[George] Henry locked Robert away in comfortable but impenetrable captivity for twenty-eight years in Cardiff Castle until Robert died at age 80, demonstrating the ruthless, unsentimental political pragmatism that defined Henry's kingship.

35
00:04:52.240 --> 00:05:01.120
[George] The campaign of Tinchabrae in 1106 was the final, climactic resolution of the division that had crippled the Norman world since the Conqueror's death.

36
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:09.120
[George] Henry sailed with a seasoned army of English infantry and Norman cavalry, displaying superior tactical generalship on the field.

37
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:19.840
[George] When Robert Curthes's frontline knights broke under the pressure of Henry's dismounted infantry, Henry captured his older brother alongside the notorious rebel Baron Robert of Bellarm.

38
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:26.080
[George] Henry's treatment of his captive brother was a calculated blend of royal luxury and absolute imprisonment.

39
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:39.880
[George] Duke Robert was housed in the royal chambers of Cardiff Castle, given fine wines and silk robes, but was guarded day and night by armed knights, dying a comfortable prisoner of state after twenty eight years of confinement.

40
00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:49.320
[George] Under Henry I, English royal government experienced an administrative revolution that transformed feudal monarchy into an organized bureaucratic state.

41
00:05:49.320 --> 00:06:03.080
[George] Appointing his brilliant Chancellor, Bishop Roger of Salisbury, Henry established the Court of Exchequer, so named from the checkered cloth resembling a chessboard used by royal clerks to calculate taxes with counters and tallies.

42
00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:16.920
[George] Henry dispatched royal circuit judges across the shires to administer uniform crown justice, severely punishing corrupt coin clippers and robbers by hanging or blinding, earning the enduring title, the Lion of Justice.

43
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:30.680
[George] In 1130, his treasury compiled the very first pipe roll, the earliest surviving detailed financial record of royal accounts in European history, laying the institutional bedrock of modern English common law.

44
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:38.840
[George] The administrative reforms orchestrated by Henry and Bishop Roger of Salisbury revolutionized the machinery of European kingship.

45
00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.800
[George] Twice a year, at Easter and Michaelmas.

46
00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:50.680
[George] The royal sheriffs of every county were summoned to the Court of Exchequer at Westminster to account for every penny of royal revenue on the great checkered cloth.

47
00:06:50.680 --> 00:07:03.560
[George] Henry's traveling circuit justices brought royal justice directly into the shires, sweeping away the arbitrary corruption of local feudal barons and replacing trial by combat with rigorous judicial inquests.

48
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] The pipe roll of 1130, a massive scroll of sheepskin parchments sewn head to tail, stands as an immortal monument to Henry's bureaucratic genius, recording royal fines, feudal reliefs and court judgments with mathematical precision.

49
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.240
[George] In November 1120, Henry's dynastic dreams reached their zenith.

50
00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:25.280
[George] King Louis V.

51
00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:35.440
[George] I of France recognized Henry's 17-year-old son, Prince William Adelin, as legitimate Duke of Normandy, securing the succession of the Anglo-Norman Empire.

52
00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:48.880
[George] On the moonlit evening of November 25, at the harbour of Barfleur in Normandy, a shipwright named Thomas Fitzstephen offered Henry a sleek, newly fitted fifty-oared vessel called the Blanche Nef, the White Ship.

53
00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:59.520
[George] Henry had already arranged his own transport, but permitted Prince William and three hundred young Norman nobles, princes, and highborn maidens to sail aboard the white ship.

54
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.960
[George] The atmosphere was a riotous, drunken Bacchanal.

55
00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:12.240
[George] Prince William distributed three casks of wine to the crew, and by the time the ship departed at midnight, the oarsmen and helmsmen were blind drunk.

56
00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:21.120
[George] Racing out of the harbor to overtake the king's ship, the white ship struck the jagged quilibuff rock at full speed, ripping open its wooden planks.

57
00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:27.680
[George] As icy water rushed in, Prince William was placed into a small rowboat and began rowing to safety.

58
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:38.400
[George] Until he heard his half-sister screaming from the sinking wreck, turning back to rescue her, frantic passengers swamped the rowboat, dragging the air of England beneath the freezing waves.

59
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:45.840
[George] Only a butcher of Rouen named Birold, clad in sheepskins, survived by clinging to the mast until dawn.

60
00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:58.800
[George] The tragedy of the white ship on November 25, 1120, was an apocalyptic catastrophe that destroyed the entire political future of the Anglo-Norman dynasty in a single hour of drunken recklessness.

61
00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:12.600
[George] Prince William Adelon, the seventeen-year-old heir to the throne, was the golden hope of the realm, beloved by his father, married to the daughter of the Count of Anjou, and acknowledged as Duke of Normandy by the French king.

62
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:27.800
[George] When the vessel struck the Quillibuff rock off Barfleur, ripping away its wooden hull, the frantic shrieks of three hundred drowning young aristocrats carried across the freezing moonlit bay to the harbor shore, where locals heard the screams but could do nothing in the dark.

63
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:34.600
[George] The loss of an entire generation of future rulers left England rudderless and doomed to future civil war.

64
00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.960
[George] When news of the disaster reached England, court officials were terrified to tell the king.

65
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:46.760
[George] Finally, a young weeping boy was sent into the royal chamber to cast himself at Henry's feet and confess the truth.

66
00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.760
[George] Henry collapsed to the stone floor in an agonizing convulsion of grief, unconscious for hours.

67
00:09:52.760 --> 00:10:00.440
[George] Chroniclers noted that from that tragic night until his death fifteen years later, King Henry I was never seen to smile again.

68
00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:08.520
[George] Devastated by the loss of his only legitimate son, Henry remarried Adeliza of Louvine, but the union produced no children.

69
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:29.960
[George] In January 1127, in an unprecedented move that shocked feudal Europe, Henry summoned all the archbishops, bishops, and barons of England to Westminster, compelling them to swear a sacred oath recognizing his widowed daughter, Empress Matilda, the former Holy Roman Empress, as his lawful heir to the crown of England and Normandy.

70
00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:35.720
[George] The psychological transformation of King Henry following the loss of his beloved son was profound.

71
00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:46.800
[George] Chroniclers noted that the once boisterous and cheerful monarch became sombre, reclusive, and haunted by insomnia, spending long nights pacing his bedchambers in solitary grief.

72
00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.920
[George] His extraordinary demand that his barons swear fealty to Empress Matilda was unprecedented in medieval Europe.

73
00:10:53.920 --> 00:11:01.360
[George] No woman had ever ruled England in her own right, and feudal custom viewed female rule as inherently unnatural and weak.

74
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:13.680
[George] The barons swore the sacred oaths under the stern, unyielding gaze of the Lion of Justice, but their whispered resentment foreshadowed the treacherous betrayal that would erupt the moment the king drew his last breath.

75
00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:30.160
[George] In late November 1135, while hunting at the Forest of Lyon near Rouen, 67-year-old King Henry fell ill following a heavy banquet of lampreys, an eel-like jawless fish that his royal physician had strictly forbidden him to eat due to his delicate digestion.

76
00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:44.560
[George] Succumbing to a violent fever caused by this surfeit of lampreys, Henry passed away on December 1, 1135, his body embalmed and shipped across the Channel to be buried in his magnificent foundation at Reading Abbey.

77
00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:51.120
[George] The moment the old Lion of Justice closed his eyes, the sacred oaths sworn to Matilda evaporated.

78
00:11:51.120 --> 00:12:01.440
[George] His charismatic nephew, Stephen of Blois, raced to London, seized the crown, and plunged England into nineteen years of catastrophic civil war known as the Anarchy.

79
00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:13.280
[George] Henry I had built an administrative engine of astonishing power, but his tragic failure to secure a peaceful succession exposed the fragile, personalized nature of medieval royal rule.

80
00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:27.760
[George] King Henry's final illness in December 1135, caused by his stubborn refusal to obey his physician's warnings against consuming rich lamprey pies, ended the reign of one of England's most formidable sovereigns at the age of 67.

81
00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:47.320
[George] Carried across the channel to his beloved foundation at Reading Abbey, Henry was interred before the high altar, mourned by his subjects who remembered his reign as a golden era of peace, order, and stern justice, where a maiden carrying a bag of gold could walk safely across the entire kingdom without fear of molestation.

82
00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:59.640
[George] King Henry I Beauclerc was the master architect of the medieval English administrative state, whose only failing was his inability to protect his dynasty from the cold, devouring waters of fate.

83
00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:15.720
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with King Stephen and Empress Matilda.

84
00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:20.040
[Rebecca] The Anarchy, 19 Winters of Blood When Christ and His Saints Slept.

85
00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.520
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

